<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsubsection xml:id="sec-all-ReducingAnxiety">
  <title>Reducing Anxiety</title>

  <p>Even if you are not dealing with anxiety that is preventing you from performing daily or required tasks or participating in desired activities, you could benefit from therapy if you experience anxiety that impacts you in a negative way. Please see <url href="https://www.midland.edu/services-resources/health-wellness/mental-health-resources.php">these resources available to MC Students</url>.</p>

  <p>If you do not wish to or are unable to pursue a therapeutic solution or if you need help while you wait for an appointment, please see <url href="https://midlandedu-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/g/personal/jkneisley_midland_edu/EZOA9fBOwulFv9NROfiYoIUBOqQROSBMk3IM_0NGN2RD8A?e=Xicvdk">this workbook from the University of Michigan for developing a plan for reducing your anxiety</url>.</p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Rewiring Your Brain</title>

    <p><em>Rewiring Math Anxiety: A Guide to Using Relaxation and Repetition to Feel Better About Math</em></p>

    <p><em>Why This Works:</em> Your brain is great at learning patterns. Right now, it might associate math with stress. But with practice, you can teach yourself to connect math with calm and control. This process combines classical conditioning, systematic desensitization, and positive reinforcement to recognize a new pattern.</p>

    <p>First, you will train your brain with a specific relaxation technique, called anchoring, to achieve a relaxed feeling in response to a phrase of your choosing. Then, you will imagine and actually put yourself in increasingly stressful scenarios while you use your anchor to induce calm. Finally, you will reward yourself when you have moved forward.</p>

    <p><em>Step 1: Notice What You're Feeling.</em> Find a quiet place to sit or lie down. Take a deep breath and check in with your body:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Checklist</title>
      <ul>
        <li>Are your shoulders tight?</li>
        <li>Is your heart beating fast?</li>
        <li>Are your thoughts swirling?</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p>Just observe. No need to fix anything yet.</p>

    <p><em>Step 2: Imagine a Safe, Relaxing Place.</em> Close your eyes and picture the most relaxing place you can imagine. This can be real or imagined. Ask yourself:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Questions to Ask</title>
      <ul>
        <li>What do I see?</li>
        <li>What do I hear?</li>
        <li>What does the air smell like?</li>
        <li>What do I feel on my skin?</li>
        <li>What tastes are there, if any?</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p><em>Example:</em> A porch swing in the mountains, light rain, pine trees, holding warm coffee...</p>

    <p>Do this daily for a few minutes. Notice how your body responds: Do you have a slower heartbeat? Looser muscles? You're making progress.</p>

    <p><em>Step 3: Choose Your Phrase.</em> Pick a short phrase to help you connect with this calm feeling. Any of these will work, but choose something personal, memorable, and natural for you:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Phrase Choices</title>
      <ul>
        <li>"I am safe."</li>
        <li>"Flurm the blibble blop."</li>
        <li>"I'm a chill potato."</li>
        <li>"Calm lives here."</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p>Repeat your phrase slowly while imagining your relaxing place. Do this daily until just saying the phrase starts to calm you.</p>

    <p><em>Step 4: Use the Phrase by Itself.</em> Practice saying your phrase without the imagery. Sit quietly, say the phrase, and observe your body.</p>

    <p>If you do not feel relaxed yet, go back to using both the image and phrase together for a few more days. You're training your brain, which takes time and kindness.</p>

    <p><em>Step 5: Add Math Slowly.</em> Now that your calming phrase works, gently practice using it in math-related situations. Start small and work your way up. If a situation is hard when you are actually doing it, try imagining it first while using your anchor.</p>

    <p><em>Start Here: Low-Stress Situations.</em> Check off when you consistently feel calm doing these with your anchor phrase:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Low-Stress Situations</title>
      <ul>
        <li>Opening my math book</li>
        <li>Looking at a review problem I already understand</li>
        <li>Skimming old class notes</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p><em>Next Steps: Mild to Moderate Stress.</em> Practice your phrase here until anxiety fades:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Mild to Moderate Stress</title>
      <ul>
        <li>Doing a few problems from a familiar homework set</li>
        <li>Watching a math video or review lesson</li>
        <li>Reading through a new concept without solving anything</li>
        <li>Trying a few problems with examples nearby</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p><em>Eventually Try: Higher-Stress Situations.</em> Use your phrase, go slow, and repeat until you feel confident:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Higher-Stress Situations</title>
      <ul>
        <li>Starting a full homework set on a new topic</li>
        <li>Working through a challenging new concept</li>
        <li>Studying for a quiz or exam</li>
        <li>Taking a practice test</li>
        <li>Going to tutoring or asking for help</li>
        <li>Attending a math class where I feel behind</li>
        <li>Taking an actual math test or exam</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p>Every time you check off a box, you're building calm and confidence. Celebrate progress, not perfection.</p>

    <p><em>Step 6: Reward Yourself.</em> After each success, even small ones, reward yourself:</p>
    <list>
      <title>Rewards</title>
      <ul>
        <li>A snack</li>
        <li>A playlist break</li>
        <li>A walk or stretch</li>
        <li>A kind word to yourself ("I showed up today")</li>
      </ul>
    </list>

    <p>This teaches your brain that math is not just safe, but satisfying and rewarding.</p>

    <p><em>Notes and Reminders:</em></p>
    <list>
      <title>Reminders</title>
      <ul>
        <li>It's normal to have setbacks.</li>
        <li>Go back a step anytime you need.</li>
        <li>Practice daily. Even 3 to 5 minutes helps.</li>
        <li>You are not bad at math; your body just needs to feel safe again.</li>
        <li>You did not develop math anxiety in a few days. It has likely been reinforced over and over for a decade or more, so it will take much longer than a few days to overcome it.</li>
        <li>You're not just learning math. You're learning emotional regulation and self-trust.</li>
      </ul>
    </list>
  </paragraphs>
</subsubsection>
